{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ועגל וכבש", :translit "v'egel v'kheves", :gloss "and a calf and a lamb"} {:he "כבש", :translit "kheves", :gloss "lamb"} {:he "לכבוש", :translit "lakhbosh", :gloss "to conquer"} {:he "מעשה עגל", :translit "ma'aseh egel", :gloss "deed of the calf"} {:he "ואיל", :translit "v'ayil", :gloss "and a ram"} {:he "כאיל", :translit "k'ayil", :gloss "like a ram"} {:he "וירקידם כמו עגל", :translit "vayarkidem k'mo egel", :gloss "and he made them dance like a calf"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: כבש (lamb) read as related to לכבוש (to conquer) — the lamb-offering serves to conquer the sin of the golden calf."} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: כאיל (like a ram) read as consonant equivalent of כאיל (as a ram), linking the dancing calf to the ram-offering."}], :frame {:text "The sin-offering calf commanded for Aaron in Leviticus 9:3 atones for the sin of the golden calf (Exodus 32). The Midrash Rabbah states that the golden calf was of one year, corresponding to Aaron's calf. The phrase וירקידם כמו עגל derives from Psalm 29:6, which describes God making the Lebanon-like forces skip like a calf.", :sources ["Leviticus 9:3" "Exodus 32" "Tanhuma Tisa 31" "Psalm 29:6"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: כבש (lamb) read as related to לכבוש (to conquer) — the lamb-offering serves to conquer the sin of the golden calf.", :by :selah, :ran? false} {:kind :other, :note "Wordplay: כאיל (like a ram) read as consonant equivalent of כאיל (as a ram), linking the dancing calf to the ram-offering.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:22:23.297297403Z", :model "glm-5.2"}